\chapter{Literals, text, characters, and bytes}
\label{ch:7}
\index{Literals, text, characters, and bytes}
\index{UTF-8!source text}
\index{Strings!embedded NUL}

Ordinary strings interpret escapes and can interpolate expressions. Escape a literal dollar in a normal string or use the raw-string form \OnxCodeTerm{r!"..."}. Triple-quoted strings cover multiline data. A character represents a Unicode scalar. A \OnxCodeTerm{b!} literal belongs to the separate byte or buffer profile, where embedded zero bytes can be meaningful. Ordinary On1x strings follow a NUL-free contract, so a binary payload should use a byte representation.

\section{Test the representation you need}
To check interpolation, compare an interpolated string with a literal expected value. For JSON text, include a backslash and a quoted character so the test distinguishes source escaping from document escaping. For binary data, assert length and individual bytes. Printing a buffer as text can hide a zero byte or invalid UTF-8 sequence.

The integer scanner recognizes explicit binary and octal forms as well as decimal spellings. A leading zero alone still means decimal. Keep spelling tests separate from range tests. The former exercise tokenization and decoding, and the latter exercise concrete representation checks. The numerical conversions and operations are covered in \cref{ch:8}.

\section{One letter, two byte representations}
Consider the pound sterling sign. Its code point in hexadecimal is A3.
Encoding that scalar in UTF-8 takes two bytes, C2 followed by A3. A byte
character literal, however, asks for the scalar's numeric value in eight
bits. The result for pound is the single byte A3. Neither answer is more
accurate than the other: one is an encoding of text and the other is a
checked conversion of a number. For a scalar with code point hex 100 the
encoding still exists, but the byte character conversion no longer fits.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
let coin = #\pound;
assert(char_codepoint(coin) == 163);
let bytes = b!"\xc2\xa3";
assert(buffer_len(bytes) == 2);
assert(buffer_at(bytes, 1) == b!#\pound);
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Place these statements inside a function. The last assertion compares two
unsigned bytes; it does not say the second byte by itself is a UTF-8
encoding of the sign. Now append zero with \OnxCodeTerm{buffer\_push}.
The length becomes three and the new last byte can be read back as zero.
Trying to obtain text with \OnxCodeTerm{buffer\_text} at that point must
fail rather than silently drop the last byte. Even before appending the
zero, success of that operation tests the absence of NUL, not the validity
of the UTF-8 sequence. These are separate checks when your input is a
packet of bytes rather than a text literal from your own program.
For instance, a one-byte buffer containing only A3 contains no NUL.
It still lacks the leading byte required to encode the pound sign as
UTF-8.

